{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And HaShem came from Sinai, etc.</b> Our Sages, may their memory be for a blessing, said (Sifrei): He went out to meet Israel from Sinai, and shone forth from Se'ir — that He opened to the children of Esav to give them the Torah; He appeared from Mount Paran — that He went also to Yishma'el. And it is difficult: why did He begin with the later event, which is ״came from Sinai,״ for this was after He had opened to Se'ir and to Paran and they did not accept, and He went out to meet Israel when He wished to give it to them? Further, one needs to know why He changed to speak in three expressions: ״came (בא),״ ״shone forth (זרח),״ ״appeared (הופיע),״ when He should have combined them all together in this manner: ״came from Sinai and from Se'ir and from Paran.״ Further, why in the case of Sinai did He put the mention of the place first and afterward what came from it, whereas in the case of Paran and Se'ir He put the coming first and afterward the place?" "<b>Indeed,</b> the matter will be explained by noting what our Sages, may their memory be for a blessing, asked: why did the patriarchs not merit — and especially Ya'akov, whose bed was complete — that HaShem give them His Torah? And they said (Bereishit Rabbah 70:9–14) that the reason is because the Shekhinah does not dwell upon fewer than sixty myriads, and the giving of the Torah required the indwelling of the Shekhinah, as we find (Exodus 19:20): ״וַיֵּרֶד ה׳ עַל הַר סִינַי.״" "And behold, one may say: do we not find that even two who sit and engage in Torah, they said in the Mishnah (Avot 3:2), the Shekhinah rests between them? To this our predecessors, who know understanding, said: what we say — that sixty myriads are required — applies to the indwelling of the totality of the Shekhinah; and what ten who sit and engage in Torah merit, or two, is one single letter that shines from it — from the One who appears — upon whoever arouses himself to cleave to holiness, each according to his capacity. And the illumination of two is not like that of three, nor three like four; but the totality of the Shekhinah does not rest except upon sixty myriads of Israel." "<b>And this</b> is the meaning of the verse's saying ״HaShem״ — meaning, the totality of the Shekhinah — ״came from Sinai״ to meet Israel. And when the Shekhinah went out to meet a chosen people, with its going out two lights went out and came: one by way of Mount Se'ir and one by way of Mount Paran, for Israel who passed through there for a known reason. Therefore He put first ״came from Sinai,״ which is the coming of the totality of the Shekhinah, and afterward He informed of the parts of its light that came by way of those places. And He was precise to say ״shone forth״ and ״appeared״ to indicate that He did not go there Himself, but rather one shining forth and one appearance from Him. And the expression הופעה is said of a small portion of holiness, in the manner our Sages, may their memory be for a blessing, said (Makkot 23b): ״the spirit of holiness appeared in our study hall,״ which is the lesser degree within the category of the indwelling of the light of the Shekhinah. And He attributed to the children of Se'ir a greater degree than the degree of Yishma'el, according to their level, for they are above Yishma'el." "<b>And with this</b> all the fine points have been resolved. Also the fact that He put the mention of Sinai before the coming, whereas with Se'ir and Paran He put the shining forth and the appearance first — for in the place of the mention of HaShem, it is the expression ״appeared״ and ״shone forth,״ and understand this. And the intention in the entire statement is to indicate their exaltedness, to multiply their blessing, for they are worthy of blessing; and this is the way of one who wishes to bless with all his heart — to arouse the matters so as to kindle the heart to bless with all his heart. And so you will find that Yitzchak did: when he wished to bless Ya'akov, he drew him near and said to him (Genesis 27:26): ״גְּשָׁה נָּא וּשְׁקָה לִּי בְּנִי״ — and this is among things that arouse." "<b>And see</b> what I explained in parashat Vayehi on the verse (Genesis 48:8) ״מִי אֵלֶּה״ according to one of the approaches: and likewise the master Moses chose to stir the love of his heart toward them from the fact that HaShem loved them and brought them up, and his holy soul would be inflamed to love the beloved of the Most High — and this is part of the perfection of his love for the Holy One, blessed be He." "<b>And you, from the myriads of holy ones.</b> The early authorities (Rashi citing the Sifre) said that the intention is to say that He brought with Him a portion of the myriads, and not all and not the majority, to inform of His humility, blessed be He. According to their words they would interpret the word ״וְאָתָה״ in place of ״וְהֵבִיא״ (and He brought), but this is not the plain sense of the word. And if they interpret ״וְאָתָה״ as ״he came,״ it is not explicit whether He brought even some of them. And perhaps they hold that the bringing of a partial number the verse does not need to state, and what we learn from the verse is that He did not bring with Him the myriads of holy ones and left them there." "<b>But apart</b> from their approach, it appears that the intention is to highlight the greatness of Israel's merit: for HaShem left an exalted host — myriads of holy ones — and came to Israel, and the meaning of ״וְאָתָה״ is: He came decisively from them and made His dwelling in their place, among His people and His inheritance. And so it was that at Sinai He commanded Israel (Exodus 25:8): ״וְעָשׂוּ לִי מִקְדָּשׁ וְשָׁכַנְתִּי בְּתוֹכָם״, and He abandoned the supernal ones. And you will find in the Zohar (Terumah 142a) how much the heavenly host suffered when HaShem fixed the dwelling of His Shekhinah among the lower ones whom He had chosen for Himself." "<b>From His right hand, a fiery law for them.</b> Meaning, the reason HaShem chose to dwell there among the lower ones — ״וְאָתָה מֵרִבְבוֹת קֹדֶשׁ״ — is because ״מִימִינוֹ אֵשׁ דָּת לָמוֹ״. This is along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Exodus Rabbah 33): on the verse ״וְיִקְחוּ לִי תְּרוּמָה״ — a parable of a king who married off his daughter, etc.; the king said: take Me too along with her. And this is the reason given here for ״וְאָתָה מֵרִבְבוֹת״ — on account of ״מִימִינוֹ אֵשׁ דָּת לָמוֹ״, meaning, for Israel." "And the verse's saying ״אֵשׁ דָּת״ and not saying ״דָּת אֵשׁ״ is to attach the word ״אֵשׁ״ to the word ״מִימִינוֹ״, to indicate that the Torah was given from the right and from the left (Sifre here). And that is its saying ״מִימִינוֹ״ — this is the right; ״אֵשׁ״ — this is the left, which is Gevurah alluded to in the aspect of fire; from the two of them, ״דָּת לָמוֹ״. And this is the sod (Deuteronomy Rabbah 3:12; Tanhuma Yitro 16) of white fire upon black fire, and it is the sod of the Torah's being called תּוּשִׁיָּה (Yevamot 62b; Yalkut Shimoni remez 23) — from the side of Gevurah, and giving might and dominion from the side of Chesed."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {3 "Resolved the acronym רז״ל as 'our Sages, may their memory be for a blessing' and ז״ל similarly embedded in 'our Sages' constructions throughout; the parenthetical Talmudic and Midrashic references are normalized to standard citation format."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:22:22.532839859Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}